@import owner.buriedoiltank.pages.PageModels.PageMeta
@param PageMeta meta
@template.layout.page(meta = meta, activeNav = "research", content = @`
<section class="service-page-hero" data-page-id="research:examples"><div class="service-shell"><p class="service-kicker">Public-source walkthroughs</p><h1>What a better research route actually changes.</h1><p>Two examples built from official agency guidance, not invented customer files. No property was searched, no request was sent, and no agency delivery is claimed in these examples.</p><nav class="research-jump" aria-label="Walkthroughs"><a href="#nassau-example">Nassau: the wrong database</a><a href="#nyc-example">NYC: the incomplete search</a><a href="/sample-brief/">Illustrative final brief</a></nav></div></section>
<section class="service-section service-section--paper" id="nassau-example"><div class="service-shell research-reading-layout"><div><p class="service-kicker">Walkthrough 01 / Nassau County</p><h2>“The bulk-storage search is empty. Where is the removal record?”</h2><p class="research-note">Illustrative question. This is not a customer's result.</p></div><div class="research-steps">
    <article><h3>Observed in official guidance</h3><p>Nassau separates homeowner small fuel-oil tank work from the bulk-storage route. Its small-tank closure system has a verification-letter function as well as scheduling.</p><a class="research-source" href="https://www.nassaucountyny.gov/3027/Environmental-Health">Nassau Environmental Health routes ↗</a><br><a class="research-source" href="https://apex5.nassaucountyny.gov/ords/f?p=340:97">Small-tank verification system ↗</a></article>
    <article><h3>How that changes our research plan</h3><p>First identify tank use, size, address, and approximate work date. For an eligible small heating-oil tank, use the verification search rather than treating the bulk-storage search as exhaustive. The scheduling workflow is not used for historical research.</p></article>
    <article><h3>What the future property brief would record</h3><dl class="research-evidence"><div><dt>If a letter is found</dt><dd>Issuer, address match, recorded action, date, document reference, and the limits of the letter.</dd></div><div><dt>If the search is empty</dt><dd>The system, search terms, date, and exact response; then the department route for historic or unmatched records.</dd></div><div><dt>Still not established</dt><dd>Present physical conditions, unrecorded work, or the absence of another tank.</dd></div></dl><a href="/research-areas/nassau-ny/">Use the full Nassau research route →</a></article>
</div></div></section>
<section class="service-section" id="nyc-example"><div class="service-shell research-reading-layout"><div><p class="service-kicker">Walkthrough 02 / New York City</p><h2>“BIS shows a permit. Is that the whole tank history?”</h2><p class="research-note">Illustrative question. No permit or report has been retrieved for a property.</p></div><div class="research-steps">
    <article><h3>Observed in official guidance</h3><p>DOB directs users to both BIS and DOB NOW. FDNY separately offers tank-specific reports, including removed or sealed tank categories. These are distinct research surfaces.</p><a class="research-source" href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/buildings/dob/find-building-data.page">DOB building-data guide ↗</a><br><a class="research-source" href="https://www.nyc.gov/site/fdny/about/resources/record-requests/records-request.page">FDNY records guide ↗</a></article>
    <article><h3>How that changes our research plan</h3><p>Resolve address, borough, and building identifiers. Search both DOB systems as applicable; preserve filing numbers and statuses. Then identify the FDNY report category needed. Its linked form requires paid physical submission, while the webpage lists a report contact email. Confirm the current channel before treating a draft email as a valid submission.</p><a class="research-source" href="https://www.nyc.gov/assets/fdny/downloads/pdf/about/public-records-fuel-tank-special-reports.pdf">FDNY report form and submission instructions ↗</a></article>
    <article><h3>What the future property brief would record</h3><dl class="research-evidence"><div><dt>Evidence in hand</dt><dd>The actual building record, its status and date, and the property identifiers used to match it.</dd></div><div><dt>Pending work</dt><dd>The named FDNY report, confirmed submission route, any approved fee, and request status.</dd></div><div><dt>Still not established</dt><dd>A permit entry alone does not resolve completion, other tanks, or environmental conditions.</dd></div></dl><a href="/research-areas/new-york-city/">Use the full NYC research route →</a></article>
</div></div></section>
<section class="service-section service-section--ink"><div class="service-shell"><p class="service-kicker">The deliverable standard</p><h2>Every conclusion points back to a source.</h2><p>Public guidance establishes where to look. A property finding requires a matched record. An agency acknowledgment establishes only that a request was received. We keep those distinctions in the case history and the customer brief.</p><div class="research-jump"><a href="/sample-brief/">See the representative brief →</a><a href="/research-areas/">Choose a local route →</a><a href="/#start-check">Send your property question →</a></div><p>Guidance reviewed September 16, 2026. Independent Oil Tank Route research; no agency endorsement.</p></div></section>
`)
